|> Exercise
    handle=_three_quarters_decimal_approximation_

    From the first three steps of the
    division of $11$ by $7$ we find $\eta \approx 1.57$:

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch5_ex_1_57_three_quarters_division.svg

    Use this approximation of $\eta$ to approximate
    
    __three-quarters the circumference__

    of a circle of radius $100$m.

    |> Solution

        Since

        $$
        \eta
        $$

        is one-quarter the circumference of a circle divided
        by its radius,

        $$
        3\eta
        $$

        is three-quarters the circumference of a circle divided
        by its radius; using $\eta \approx 1.57$, $3\eta$ is approximately

        $$
        3 \times 1.57 = 3\times (1.5 + 0.07) = 3 \times 1.5 + 3\times 0.07
        $$
        $$
        =\,\,4.5 + 0.21
        $$
        $$
        =\,\,4.71
        $$

        so three-quarters the circumference of a circle of radius $100$m
        will be 

        $$
        \approx 4.71 \times 100\te{m} = 471\te{m}.
        $$

        |> SolutionNote
            The following table gives approximation ratios
            going from $n = 1$ to $n = 4$ quarters, similarly
            computed, that one might as well remember:
            $$
            \begin{array}{c|c|c}
            \,\,\,\,n\,\,\,\, & n(1.5 + 0.07) & \,\,\,\,\approx n\eta\,\,\,\,\Rule{0pt}{0.8em}{0.5em} \\ \hline
            1 & \up{1.2} 1.5 + 0.07 & 1.57\\
            2 & \up{1.2} 3.0 + 0.14 & 3.14\\
            3 & \up{1.2} 4.5 + 0.21 & 4.71\\
            4 & \up{1.2} 6.0 + 0.28 & 6.28
            \end{array}
            $$
            !! For example,
            !! $$
            !! 6.28
            !! $$
            !! is an approximation to the circumference of a circle
            !! divided by its radius, since $6.28 = 4 \times 1.57$.

        |> SolutionNote

            While

            $$
            \Large{11\over 7}
            $$

            is about half-a-part-in-a-thousand

            __too large__

            compared to the true value of $\eta$, 
            the approximations above just so turn out to
            be (really nearly quite exactly) half-a-part-in-a-thousand

            __too small__

            due to the fact that the the long division of $11$ by
            $7$ is terminated early. But if we pursue the division
            for just one more step, which adds a ‘$1$’ to the
            quotient...
            
            |> Image
                src=images/svg_ch5_ex_1_57_three_quarters_division_one_more.svg
            
            ...we obtain the modified approximation...

            $$
            \eta \approx 1.571
            $$

            ...that is, for its part, an _overestimate_ of $\eta$.
            (Whereas $$1.57$$ is still an _underestimate_.) From there,
            any further digits that are added to the quotient only
            worsen the approximation, since we are already overshooting!

        |> SolutionNote

            In fact, the true value of $\eta$ is roughly half-way
            between

            $$
            1.57
            $$

            and

            $$
            {11\over 7} = 1.571428\dots
            $$

            being

            $$
            \,\eta = 1.570796\dots
            $$

            per the calculator. (That knows this value by heart.)
            Taking into account that

            $$
            1.570796
            $$

            is _basically_

            $$
            1.5708
            $$

            gives us the following improved approximations of
            $\eta$, $2\eta$, $3\eta$, and $4\eta$, based on adding
            multiples of ‘$08$’ to the tail end of our previous
            approximations:

            $$
            \begin{array}{c|c|c}
            \,\,\,\,n\,\,\,\, & n(1.5 + 0.07 + 0.0008) & \,\,\,\,\approx n\eta\,\,\,\,\Rule{0pt}{0.8em}{0.5em} \\ \hline
            1 & \up{1.2} 1.5 + 0.07 + 0.0008 & 1.5708\\
            2 & \up{1.2} 3.0 + 0.14 + 0.0016 & 3.1416\\
            3 & \up{1.2} 4.5 + 0.21 + 0.0024 & 4.7124\\
            4 & \up{1.2} 6.0 + 0.28 + 0.0032 & 6.2832
            \end{array}
            $$

            ...with these approximations being good to _three
            parts in a million_. (Improved, namely, from _half a
            part in a thousand_.)
